WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:16.980
Now, this is a guess, because I wasn’t there,
and we don’t have any clear records of this

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:23.269
period of history, but at some point, in the
early history of life on earth, it’s very

00:00:23.269 --> 00:00:27.410
likely nothing could move.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:33.450
Cells existed where they were, they got sloshed
around through physical processes, and survived

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.751
when they ended up, by chance, in some place
that had the necessary chemicals for them

00:00:37.751 --> 00:00:40.430
to continue their lives and reproduce.

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:45.240
If that didn’t happen they just died.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:52.550
But since food is never evenly distributed
in our world, and also sometimes you are food,

00:00:52.550 --> 00:01:00.020
being able to move is fantastic, and so it
has been selected for pretty intensely so

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:06.359
that, now, in the microcosmos, almost everything
moves.

00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:11.479
But before we get to how they do it, we have
to confront a reality.

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:17.000
You need to forget everything you know about
swimming.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.549
Things do not work the same when you are tiny.

00:01:20.549 --> 00:01:27.960
You and I are constantly moving through a
fluid, it’s just hard to feel it, air.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:34.490
It’s barely there, until you stick your
hand out of a car window on the highway.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.460
Then you feel that fluid.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:44.600
But for a tiny grain of pollen, air is a thick
fluid that can keep it airborne for days.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:51.579
Now sink that tiny grain of pollen into far
more viscous water, and it’s basically in

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:54.299
a sticky, glue.

00:01:54.299 --> 00:02:00.850
Imagine being completely submerged in honey
and you have a vague idea of what it’s like

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.459
swimming in the microcosmos.

00:02:03.459 --> 00:02:10.459
Which is why, in that microscopic world, when
something stops moving, it just stops.

00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:15.280
You and I, if we push through the water, we’ll
coast for a bit as our momentum carries us

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:22.510
forward, but for a single cell, the viscosity
of the water overcomes that inertia instantly.

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:28.519
This can lead to organisms looking as if they
are moving somewhat unnaturally to our eyes, which is

00:02:28.519 --> 00:02:33.099
we think why we’ve had several people have
ask us if our footage is sped up.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:39.019
But, no, unless it says so on-screen, all
of our clips are in real time.

00:02:39.019 --> 00:02:40.860
These little folks can just move!

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:47.170
And they need to move...to search for food,
to avoid predators, to get into or out of

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:52.580
sunlight, to move toward chemicals they need,
or away from chemicals that poison them.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:59.980
And the wild thing is, with all of the diversity
of microscopic life single celled eukaryotes

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:06.290
basically all move around in three or maybe
four different ways.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:11.379
How organisms move is so important, and so
obvious when they’re observed, that protozoans,

00:03:11.379 --> 00:03:18.079
which is the general name for single-celled
eukaryotes, are actually loosely classified

00:03:18.079 --> 00:03:19.500
by their style of movement.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:25.870
We’ve got ciliates, which move using cilia,
flagellates, which move using flagella, and

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:30.359
amoeboids, which move using pseudopodia.

00:03:30.359 --> 00:03:37.140
Oh...and then there’s Sporozoa…which we
can’t show you a picture of, for reasons

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:39.519
that will become clear.

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:47.069
Sporozoa almost never move, and when they
do it’s in a very weird limited way.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:54.230
And the reason we can’t show you a picture
of one is that they are, every one them, parasites.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:04:00.799
Many of them live inside of, and cause disease
in animals so we choose to, y’know, not

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:02.260
keep them around.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:07.489
But, overall, among eukaryotes we’ve got
cilia, flagella, and pseudopodia.

00:04:07.489 --> 00:04:15.129
And we’re going to start with the shapeshifters
of the Microcosmos, amoeba!

00:04:15.129 --> 00:04:21.049
These cells can change their shape as they
wish, allowing them to extend parts of themselves

00:04:21.049 --> 00:04:24.669
into features called pseudopodia.

00:04:24.669 --> 00:04:30.400
These extend in the direction they want to
move, and then solidify as the cell moves

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.960
into the newly occupied space.

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:40.289
Pseudopodia is latin for, basically, false
feet, and these extensions which are half

00:04:40.289 --> 00:04:46.830
reaching arm, half cell body are how these
crawlers move and hunt.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:52.330
The secret here is that the cytoplasm of an
amoeba can be easily changed from a fluid

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:56.030
state into a solid state and back again.

00:04:56.030 --> 00:05:03.099
When an amoeba moves, liquid cytoplasm flows
through the center, up to the tip of the pseudopod

00:05:03.099 --> 00:05:09.630
and then gushes to the sides where it becomes
more of a solid gel allowing the cell to lock

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.610
into its new location.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:18.940
Predatory amoebae create these extensions
in all directions to trap prey between them,

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:26.440
once the prey organism is surrounded by the
pseudopodia the amoeba simply swallows it.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:33.819
The two other main mechanisms of eukaryotic
movement, cilia and flagella, are more common,

00:05:33.819 --> 00:05:38.889
and they’re actually really similar, both
functionally and structurally at the molecular

00:05:38.889 --> 00:05:39.889
level.

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:44.979
Though they look like rods sticking through
the surface, they’re actually extensions

00:05:44.979 --> 00:05:50.690
of the cell membrane wrapped around rigid
tubes called microtubules that are anchored

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.770
in place.

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:55.360
However, hydrodynamically they’re very different.

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:03.139
There are usually an easily countable number
of flagella per cell whereas ciliated organisms

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:05.410
have huge numbers of cilia.

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:10.850
The word cilia actually comes from the latin
word for eyelash, which makes sense as you

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:16.110
can sometimes see them ringing a cell as our
eyelashes ring our eyes.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:22.349
But, in those cases, the cilia actually often
cover the entire cell, they’re just harder

00:06:22.349 --> 00:06:27.860
to spot against the background of all the
stuff on the inside.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:31.900
Cilia move organisms by beating in waves over
their surface.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:39.910
A cilium, which is the singular of cilia because...latin...has
only two possible positions.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:44.581
During the effective stroke, the cilium sticks
out perpendicular to the cell, and during

00:06:44.581 --> 00:06:49.290
recovery it folds back towards the cell’s
surface.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:55.800
In most ciliates the cilia are arranged in
rows and the cilia of a row don't move all

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:01.520
together at once; when some of the cilia are
in the effective stroke some are in recovery

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:07.120
stroke, and in some ciliates you can see the
"wave" pattern this creates.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:15.629
These waves are basically grabbing onto the
sticky viscous water, yanking the cell through.

00:07:15.629 --> 00:07:22.130
One added complication here, some ciliates
have dense bundles of cilia called cirri.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:29.319
Cirri are used to "walk" on a solid substrate
rather than pulling the organism through the

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:30.319
water.

00:07:30.319 --> 00:07:36.349
Though it’s the same structure, it’s a
bit of a different system of movement.

00:07:36.349 --> 00:07:42.910
Now on to eukaryotic flagella.

00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:49.240
The motion of most flagella is characterized
by this long, wave-like beating pattern beginning

00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:53.639
from the base of the flagella and moving out
to the tip.

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:59.580
This is Phacus longicauda, we recorded this
footage in phase contrast which makes the

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:05.080
transparent parts of the cell more visible
so you can see the beating flagellum better.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:08.770
Can you see it at the tip of the cell?

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:15.330
Phacus longicauda is a photosynthetic flagellate...it
uses sunlight to produce sugar.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:20.159
You can see the chloroplasts in the cell like
green peas.

00:08:20.159 --> 00:08:24.289
And the round transparent part at the middle
of the cell is a starch-like carbohydrate

00:08:24.289 --> 00:08:28.259
storage unit called paramylon.

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:37.919
Now, of course, as always, the deeper you
look the more confusing and amazing things

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:38.919
get.

00:08:38.919 --> 00:08:44.839
Diatoms are sometimes completely non-motile,
but some species can also move by excreting

00:08:44.839 --> 00:08:49.760
mucus through their cell, slowly propelling
themselves over a substrate.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:58.260
And finally, we said nothing in this entire
video about the marvelous movements of prokaryotes

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.079
like bacteria.

00:09:00.079 --> 00:09:07.300
And they have systems every bit, if not more
ingenious than their more complex eukaryotic

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:08.300
friends.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:15.980
But they’re also tiny...so tiny that these
structures cannot be observed by microscopes

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:18.050
like the ones we use.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:23.850
So we just have to be content knowing that
they use structures that function similarly

00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:30.160
to the structures eukaryotes use, but that
are made of completely different stuff.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:38.100
And all of these chemical structures, eukaryotic
and prokaryotic, so complex and marvelous,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:43.030
were selected for through billions of years
of evolution.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:51.410
And almost shockingly, just a few extremely
effective systems of locomotion were uncovered.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:58.459
The result...we can watch them, these little
balls of chemicals working to get what they

00:09:58.459 --> 00:10:10.139
need...they are each a little soup that wants...look
at them wanting.

00:10:10.139 --> 00:10:15.480
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.480
us.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:20.930
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James, check out Jam and Germs

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:21.930
on Instagram.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:28.540
And if you want more from us, we’ve got
a few videos over at youtube.com/microcosmos

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.290
and we put out a new one every week

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.120
We’ve been doing a pretty good job of keeping
on top of it.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.640
So, thanks for liking what we do.

